00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:10,260
- [Instructor] The next
concept that is very important

3
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:14,733
is that of marginal rate of substitution.

4
00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:21,210
The concept of marginal
rate of substitution

5
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:25,320
is very important and it is based

6
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:27,870
on what we have already learned before

7
00:00:27,870 --> 00:00:30,273
in terms of marginal utility.

8
00:00:31,380 --> 00:00:36,380
And so let's first say what
conceptually MRS means.

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,000
MRS is the slope of one
indifference curve, okay?

10
00:00:47,193 --> 00:00:49,740
It's a slope of the indifference curve

11
00:00:49,740 --> 00:00:54,030
at a certain combination of x1 and x2.

12
00:00:54,030 --> 00:00:55,890
Let me say it again.

13
00:00:55,890 --> 00:00:57,870
So suppose you have certain combination

14
00:00:57,870 --> 00:01:01,230
of good one and good two, x1, x2

15
00:01:01,230 --> 00:01:05,220
and you know that through
that particular bundle,

16
00:01:05,220 --> 00:01:08,193
there is an indifference
curve going through.

17
00:01:09,870 --> 00:01:12,330
The relative preference between good one

18
00:01:12,330 --> 00:01:17,330
and good two is given, to a large extent,

19
00:01:18,210 --> 00:01:21,480
by how steep is that indifference curve?

20
00:01:21,480 --> 00:01:23,460
If it's very steep, it means

21
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:28,460
that this person values good
one a lot relative to good two.

22
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:30,900
And if it's very flat,

23
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:35,900
means that this consumer
values good two highly relative

24
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:39,090
to good one.

25
00:01:39,090 --> 00:01:41,820
So MRS contains, in that sense,

26
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:46,170
a lot of information but
mathematically, but okay,

27
00:01:46,170 --> 00:01:48,390
so we know that contains
a lot of information

28
00:01:48,390 --> 00:01:51,360
but we just need to know
what is the easiest way,

29
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:55,320
what is the correct way
to find it mathematically

30
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:59,130
every time we have a
specific utility function.

31
00:01:59,130 --> 00:02:02,460
And that is what this slide is about.

32
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:07,460
The MRS, also known as the
marginal rate of substitution.

33
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:10,740
And we are learning that is the slope

34
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:12,180
of an indifference curve.

35
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:17,180
And this is why it's dx2/dx1

36
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:22,850
in this highlighted equation
is gonna be given by the ratio

37
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:28,460
of MU1 and MU2 with a
minus sign before that.

38
00:02:30,600 --> 00:02:33,270
So how do we know this last equality

39
00:02:33,270 --> 00:02:38,270
that the MRS equals
minus MU1 divided by MU2?

40
00:02:41,400 --> 00:02:44,460
Well, this is what we're gonna prove here.

41
00:02:44,460 --> 00:02:47,610
We know first that an
indifference curve is such

42
00:02:47,610 --> 00:02:51,300
that the satisfaction
level is constant at some,

43
00:02:51,300 --> 00:02:53,853
let's say k value.

44
00:02:54,840 --> 00:02:58,410
We can use something that you've learned

45
00:02:58,410 --> 00:03:02,700
in your calculus classes core
as the total differentiation

46
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:07,700
and total differentiation
is taking this equation

47
00:03:07,890 --> 00:03:11,340
and taking the total
differentiation of it.

48
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:14,160
And that total differentiation

49
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:17,400
of it is written like this.

50
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:20,640
First is the change of x1

51
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:25,290
multiply by how sensitive
U is with respect to x1,

52
00:03:25,290 --> 00:03:28,830
which is given by the
partial derivative of U

53
00:03:28,830 --> 00:03:33,830
with respect to x1 plus how
sensitive is D2 function

54
00:03:36,510 --> 00:03:39,210
with respect to the second good

55
00:03:39,210 --> 00:03:44,210
times how big the change
of that good two is,

56
00:03:44,280 --> 00:03:47,193
which is represented here again by dx2.

57
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:51,210
And that should be equal
to the total change

58
00:03:51,210 --> 00:03:54,210
in the utility that we will call here dk.

59
00:03:54,210 --> 00:03:59,210
However, dk must be zero
because k is constant.

60
00:03:59,430 --> 00:04:03,693
So dk cannot be different
than zero, it must be zero.

61
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:09,620
That is that we can use this knowledge,

62
00:04:11,940 --> 00:04:15,750
we can use this knowledge or
this fact into this equation

63
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:17,550
but that's not the only thing we can use.

64
00:04:17,550 --> 00:04:20,430
We also need to notice that
the partial with respect

65
00:04:20,430 --> 00:04:24,960
to x1 and x2 are simply
the marginal utilities.

66
00:04:24,960 --> 00:04:29,960
So we can rewrite this
guy like this using dk0

67
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:34,667
and using that the derivatives
of U with respect to x1

68
00:04:34,667 --> 00:04:39,603
and x2 simply are MU1
and MU2 respectively.

69
00:04:41,070 --> 00:04:43,320
So we have this equation.

70
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:48,320
From this equation, we can
form or solve for dx2, dx1

71
00:04:52,740 --> 00:04:56,910
which is the slope of
the indifference curve.

72
00:04:56,910 --> 00:05:01,217
To do that, we need to
divide both sides by dx1.

73
00:05:04,860 --> 00:05:06,900
And after we have done that,

74
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:10,353
we can solve indeed for dx2/dx1.

75
00:05:11,490 --> 00:05:15,003
If we do that, we will get this equation,

76
00:05:17,010 --> 00:05:19,143
which is exactly what we wanted to show.

77
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:24,690
Let's just for the sake of comprehension

78
00:05:24,690 --> 00:05:27,003
do that on the iPad as well.

79
00:05:30,690 --> 00:05:35,690
Marginal rate of substitution.

80
00:05:38,700 --> 00:05:39,533
Okay?

81
00:05:42,330 --> 00:05:43,680
Let's put it in a nicer place.

82
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:44,880
Okay, cool.

83
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:49,380
So we will try to prove that the slope

84
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:53,590
of a indifference curve

85
00:05:56,490 --> 00:06:01,490
is the same as minus MU1 divided by MU2.

86
00:06:03,090 --> 00:06:05,943
This is what we want to prove.

87
00:06:06,900 --> 00:06:11,700
And to do that, we will
start again by saying, okay,

88
00:06:11,700 --> 00:06:13,563
along one indifference curve,

89
00:06:15,030 --> 00:06:19,263
the utility level is fixed at a level k.

90
00:06:21,990 --> 00:06:24,423
Second, we will say,

91
00:06:26,310 --> 00:06:29,340
we can take total differentiation of this

92
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:32,740
and this is going to be the impact of x1

93
00:06:34,740 --> 00:06:39,240
on U times the change
of x1 plus the impact

94
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:44,240
of x2 on utility times the change in x2.

95
00:06:45,690 --> 00:06:48,273
And they will be equal
to the change in utility.

96
00:06:49,140 --> 00:06:51,993
However, as we have seen before, oops.

97
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:58,020
However, as we have seen before,
this guy here must be zero.

98
00:06:58,020 --> 00:07:03,020
And these guys here are
MU1 and MU2 respectively.

99
00:07:04,920 --> 00:07:07,500
And therefore, we can
rewrite this equation

100
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:12,500
as MU1 dx1 plus MU2 times dx2 equals zero.

101
00:07:18,060 --> 00:07:23,060
So from this point, we can
do, we can divide both sides

102
00:07:24,540 --> 00:07:29,187
by the dx1 and we will have this.

103
00:07:35,100 --> 00:07:37,320
From this point, we can subtract MU1

104
00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:41,880
from both sides and we
will have the following.

105
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:44,280
Well, we can subtract MU1 from both sides

106
00:07:44,280 --> 00:07:48,510
and also divide both sides by MU2.

107
00:07:48,510 --> 00:07:51,183
And we will get this.

108
00:08:01,470 --> 00:08:03,720
And this is exactly what we wanted.

109
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:08,720
So we have been able to
show what we're looking for.

110
00:08:11,310 --> 00:08:16,310
Okay, let us now do some
examples of calculating MRS.

111
00:08:19,230 --> 00:08:21,630
Let's say we're going to do,

112
00:08:21,630 --> 00:08:26,130
we will do similar functions
that we have just before.

113
00:08:26,130 --> 00:08:29,373
So let's start with some Cobb-Douglas.

114
00:08:34,890 --> 00:08:39,890
Suppose the utility function
is x1 squared times x2.

115
00:08:41,790 --> 00:08:45,663
So MRS, remember is, oops.

116
00:08:47,258 --> 00:08:52,217
MRS, okay, is minus MU1 divided by MU2.

117
00:08:55,151 --> 00:09:00,151
And MU1 is the partial
of U with respect to x1

118
00:09:00,390 --> 00:09:05,310
and MU2 is the partial
of x, sorry, partial of U

119
00:09:05,310 --> 00:09:07,803
with respect to x2.

120
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:13,880
So with that in mind, we can
calculate the partial of X

121
00:09:15,090 --> 00:09:18,960
of U with respect to
x1, which would be two

122
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:21,750
x1 times x2.

123
00:09:21,750 --> 00:09:25,320
And the partial of U with respect to x2

124
00:09:25,320 --> 00:09:30,320
or MU2 would be x1 squared times one.

125
00:09:30,660 --> 00:09:34,680
So we get this, we can
simplify a little bit

126
00:09:34,680 --> 00:09:39,680
and we will get two
times x2 divided by x1.

127
00:09:41,070 --> 00:09:45,870
So that would be our MRS
for the first function.

128
00:09:45,870 --> 00:09:47,823
Let's do another example.

129
00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:57,300
Example two, finding MRS.

130
00:09:57,300 --> 00:10:01,773
This time for the utility function equals.

131
00:10:04,650 --> 00:10:05,800
Oops, sorry about that.

132
00:10:07,020 --> 00:10:12,020
Utility function equals
x1 times x2 square root.

133
00:10:15,120 --> 00:10:15,953
Okay?

134
00:10:15,953 --> 00:10:19,790
So in this case, we will say that again,

135
00:10:22,821 --> 00:10:25,030
MRS equals

136
00:10:27,750 --> 00:10:32,070
minus MU1 divided by MU2.

137
00:10:32,070 --> 00:10:34,980
Let's not write the whole
partial derivative this time

138
00:10:34,980 --> 00:10:36,510
because it's not necessary.

139
00:10:36,510 --> 00:10:40,740
We know that MU1 is a partial
of U with respect to x1.

140
00:10:40,740 --> 00:10:45,740
So in this case, we can
directly write x2 square root

141
00:10:45,810 --> 00:10:50,310
because that is the partial
of x of U with respect to x1

142
00:10:50,310 --> 00:10:53,910
and the minus sign here is simply the one

143
00:10:53,910 --> 00:10:55,530
that is coming from there.

144
00:10:55,530 --> 00:10:57,000
Okay?

145
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,400
And with that in mind,

146
00:10:59,400 --> 00:11:02,700
we can now calculate the partial

147
00:11:02,700 --> 00:11:07,680
of U with respect to x2, which would be x1

148
00:11:07,680 --> 00:11:12,063
times 1/2 x2 minus 1/2.

149
00:11:13,260 --> 00:11:18,260
So it's interesting, but now
this can be also simplified.

150
00:11:28,650 --> 00:11:31,980
The way we simplify this is by noting

151
00:11:31,980 --> 00:11:36,917
that having 1/2 in the
denominator is the same

152
00:11:40,020 --> 00:11:42,480
as having two in the numerator.

153
00:11:42,480 --> 00:11:43,800
That's the first thing.

154
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:47,670
The second thing is that we have x2 power

155
00:11:47,670 --> 00:11:51,480
to something both in the
denominator and the numerator

156
00:11:51,480 --> 00:11:54,600
and we can bring those
together to the numerator.

157
00:11:54,600 --> 00:11:59,290
So it would be x to power to 1/2 plus 1/2

158
00:12:01,590 --> 00:12:05,460
and that plus 1/2 will come from this.

159
00:12:05,460 --> 00:12:10,460
It would be x to 1/2 from
what we already knew.

160
00:12:12,270 --> 00:12:16,650
It's in the numerator minus
because there is also something

161
00:12:16,650 --> 00:12:20,460
in the denominator,
1/2 because 1/2, sorry,

162
00:12:20,460 --> 00:12:25,230
minus minus 1/2 because
minus 1/2 is the exponent

163
00:12:25,230 --> 00:12:27,183
in the denominator for x2.

164
00:12:29,130 --> 00:12:34,130
And we know that in the
denominator, we also have x1.

165
00:12:34,770 --> 00:12:39,770
So all in all, this will
simplify to two times, sorry,

166
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:45,960
minus two times x2 divided by x1.

167
00:12:47,940 --> 00:12:49,889
You might be thinking, wait a second,

168
00:12:49,889 --> 00:12:52,413
where have we seen this before?

169
00:12:53,310 --> 00:12:56,220
And the answer would be
in the previous example.

170
00:12:56,220 --> 00:12:58,770
How come these two utility functions,

171
00:12:58,770 --> 00:13:02,320
this guy here, this guy here

172
00:13:03,180 --> 00:13:05,470
and this guy here

173
00:13:08,040 --> 00:13:10,020
have the same MRS?

174
00:13:10,020 --> 00:13:13,140
Well, it is because these
two functions actually

175
00:13:13,140 --> 00:13:15,840
are representing the same preferences.

176
00:13:15,840 --> 00:13:17,160
They can be written

177
00:13:17,160 --> 00:13:21,000
as an increasing
transformation of the other.

178
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:24,240
And as we had anticipated before,

179
00:13:24,240 --> 00:13:27,360
if they represent the same preferences,

180
00:13:27,360 --> 00:13:30,690
they will have the same indifference maps,

181
00:13:30,690 --> 00:13:34,200
they will have the same slopes
for the indifference curves,

182
00:13:34,200 --> 00:13:36,480
meaning they will have the same MRS.

183
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:40,440
So all these coincidence is
actually not a coincidence,

184
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:43,890
it's a mathematical
implication of the definitions

185
00:13:43,890 --> 00:13:48,870
and the math concepts that are applied

186
00:13:48,870 --> 00:13:51,120
to indifference curves,

187
00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:56,120
uniqueness of utility functions and MRS.

188
00:13:59,640 --> 00:14:04,110
Finally, we will do an
example of a function

189
00:14:04,110 --> 00:14:08,100
that is of perfect substitutes.

190
00:14:08,100 --> 00:14:10,200
So we're gonna do it here.

191
00:14:10,200 --> 00:14:14,473
Example three, we're
going to do utility, x1

192
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:20,780
plus let's say five times x2.

193
00:14:21,180 --> 00:14:25,047
So for utility x1 plus five times x2,

194
00:14:28,980 --> 00:14:32,670
we can calculate again also the MRS.

195
00:14:32,670 --> 00:14:37,670
And the MRS would be, as
always, MU1 minus ahead of that

196
00:14:38,850 --> 00:14:42,720
or minus MU1 divided by MU2.

197
00:14:42,720 --> 00:14:46,950
MU1, in this perfect complements
preference, is very simple.

198
00:14:46,950 --> 00:14:48,990
It's simply one.

199
00:14:48,990 --> 00:14:52,530
So it would be minus one in the numerator

200
00:14:52,530 --> 00:14:55,650
and the MU2 would be simply five.

201
00:14:55,650 --> 00:14:57,333
So in this case,

202
00:14:59,790 --> 00:15:01,980
we will simply have

203
00:15:01,980 --> 00:15:06,033
that the MRS is minus 1/5.

204
00:15:07,440 --> 00:15:12,440
This is an important result

205
00:15:15,150 --> 00:15:20,070
because it's actually generalizable
for perfect complements.

206
00:15:20,070 --> 00:15:24,920
Okay, so we're gonna
write here, MRS for...

207
00:15:26,460 --> 00:15:27,900
Actually, we're gonna write it with white

208
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:30,330
so we keep our convention

209
00:15:30,330 --> 00:15:33,960
of having white for notes
and yellow for examples.

210
00:15:33,960 --> 00:15:38,960
So the MRS

211
00:15:39,390 --> 00:15:44,120
for perfect substitutes

212
00:15:49,620 --> 00:15:53,410
is always

213
00:15:57,750 --> 00:15:58,773
constant.

214
00:16:01,500 --> 00:16:03,480
Why is that?

215
00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:07,770
This is actually part of the definition

216
00:16:07,770 --> 00:16:11,400
or this is another way to
define perfect substitutes.

217
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:15,030
Perfect substitutes have the same rate

218
00:16:15,030 --> 00:16:18,540
of substitution regardless
of the combination

219
00:16:18,540 --> 00:16:22,110
or regardless of the
bundle we are considering.

220
00:16:22,110 --> 00:16:27,110
So by definition, the perfect
substitutes preferences

221
00:16:27,180 --> 00:16:29,550
will have a constant MRS.

222
00:16:29,550 --> 00:16:31,920
If you remember, the indifference curves

223
00:16:31,920 --> 00:16:35,430
for perfect substitutes preferences

224
00:16:35,430 --> 00:16:39,423
were having indifference curves
that would look like this.

225
00:16:41,760 --> 00:16:45,840
They were all parallel lines

226
00:16:49,920 --> 00:16:52,360
and that will make them

227
00:16:55,140 --> 00:16:57,810
have indeed the same slope

228
00:16:57,810 --> 00:17:00,030
of their four indifferent curves,

229
00:17:00,030 --> 00:17:02,520
meaning the MRS might must be constant

230
00:17:02,520 --> 00:17:07,513
and the same for any
combination of x1 and x2.

231
00:17:09,420 --> 00:17:11,580
This concludes our discussion

232
00:17:11,580 --> 00:17:15,150
of preferences and utility.

233
00:17:15,150 --> 00:17:20,150
And we will then discuss how the ideas

234
00:17:20,580 --> 00:17:23,580
of budget constraint,
as well as preferences

235
00:17:23,580 --> 00:17:28,580
and utility combine in what
we call the consumer choice,

236
00:17:28,650 --> 00:17:33,540
how the consumer uses their wallet

237
00:17:33,540 --> 00:17:37,530
and their preferences or
their brain to find out

238
00:17:37,530 --> 00:17:40,860
what is the best option they can afford,

239
00:17:40,860 --> 00:17:42,158
what is the best thing,

240
00:17:42,158 --> 00:17:44,760
what is the best affordable thing

241
00:17:44,760 --> 00:17:46,994
